course: dictionary
lesson: 14157
title: One word, three meanings
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Some words have more than one meaning. The meaning that fits depends on the context, which means the words and sentences around it. «I'll have to ask the missus before we book the trip» tells you the missus is someone who makes decisions with the speaker, so it must mean a wife or partner.
explain: «Excuse me, missus, you dropped your glove.» Here the speaker does not know the woman, so missus is a casual way to get her attention. The word is informal, so you hear it in chatty, everyday talk, not in school essays.
explain: Be careful with missus. Some women find it old-fashioned or even rude when a stranger uses it. If you are not sure, use the woman's name or say «excuse me».
fact: Context means the words and sentences around a word.
fact: «Missus» is informal. You hear it in everyday talk in the UK, Ireland, Australia and New Zealand.
fact: «The missus» means a man's wife or partner, as in «I'll have to ask the missus».
fact: «Missus» can also be a casual way to speak to a woman you don't know.
fact: «Missus» can be a short form of Mrs, but it is informal, so keep it for chatty talk.
exercise: «I'll have to ask the missus before we book the trip.» In this sentence, who is the missus? | *A man's wife or partner | A shop worker | A school head teacher | A bus driver
exercise: «Excuse me, missus, you dropped your glove.» What does missus mean here? | *An informal way to speak to a woman you don't know | A kind of hat | The name of a shop | A name for a dog
exercise: «Missus Brown lives next door.» In this sentence, missus is informal for which word? | *Mrs | Missing | Moose | Mister
test: «Me missus says dinner is ready.» What does me missus mean? | A street name | *My wife | My sister | A dinner plate
test: Is it always polite to call a stranger missus? | Yes, it is always the most polite way | Yes, because it means Mrs | No, because it is a number | *No, some women find it old-fashioned or rude
exam: Which meaning fits «Need a hand, missus?» said by a shopkeeper to a customer? | A type of bag | *A way to address a woman you don't know | Mrs with a name | A man's wife
